  "row_count": 19,
  "notes": {
    "raw": "\\*Enthalpy of fusion and heat capacity of liquid estimated. \nPhase changes: 600.65 K, melting point of Pb; 1.147 kcal/mol. \n1386.5 K, melting point of PbS; $\\Delta H^{\\circ} = 4.5$ kcal/mol. \nHeat capacity (cal/mol·K) and enthalpy (kcal/mol) equations: \n<table><tr><td>298.15-1386.5 K:</td><td> $Cp^o$  = 11.534 + 1.806x $10^{-3}$ T - 0.223x $10^5$ T $^{-2}$ </td></tr><tr><td rowspan=\"3\">1386.5-1600 K:</td><td> $H^o$ -  $H_{298}^o$  = 11.534x $10^{-3}$ T + 0.903x $10^{-6}$ T $^2$  + 0.223x $10^2$ T $^{-1}$  - 3.594</td></tr><tr><td> $Cp^o$  = 16.000</td></tr><tr><td> $H^o$ -  $H_{298}^o$  = 16.000x $10^{-3}$ T - 3.534</td></tr></table>\nReaction equations (kcal/mol): \n<table><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">298.15-600.65 K:</td><td> $\\Delta Hr^{\\circ} = -39.612 + 2.512 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T} - 1.021 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{T}^{2} + 29.550 \\text{T}^{-1}$ </td></tr><tr><td> $\\Delta Gr^{\\circ} = -39.612 - 2.512 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T} \\ln T + 1.021 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{T}^{2} + 14.775 \\text{T}^{-1} + 37.321 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T}$ </td></tr><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">600.65-1386.5 K:</td><td> $\\Delta Hr^{\\circ} = -39.960 - 0.052 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T} + 0.956 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{T}^{2} + 46.150 \\text{T}^{-1}$ </td></tr><tr><td> $\\Delta Gr^{\\circ} = -39.960 + 0.052 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T} \\ln T - 0.956 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{T}^{2} + 23.075 \\text{T}^{-1} + 22.660 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T}$ </td></tr><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">1386.5-1600 K:</td><td> $\\Delta Hr^{\\circ} = -39.900 + 4.414 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T} + 0.053 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{T}^{2} + 23.850 \\text{T}^{-1}$ </td></tr><tr><td> $\\Delta Gr^{\\circ} = -39.900 - 4.414 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T} \\ln T - 0.053 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{T}^{2} + 11.925 \\text{T}^{-1} + 53.681 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T}$ </td></tr></table>\nSources: Enthalpy of formation at 298 K based on Kiukkola (69) and Thompson (117). Low-temperature heat capacities and entropy at 298 K based on Anderson (3), Parkinson (95), and Kelley (63). High-temperature data based on Thompson (116) and Blachnik (11). Temperature and enthalpy of fusion and heat capacity of liquid are those estimated by Chase (18). ",
    "lines_raw": [
      "\\*Enthalpy of fusion and heat capacity of liquid estimated.",
      "Phase changes: 600.65 K, melting point of Pb; 1.147 kcal/mol.",
      "1386.5 K, melting point of PbS; $\\Delta H^{\\circ} = 4.5$ kcal/mol.",
      "Heat capacity (cal/mol·K) and enthalpy (kcal/mol) equations:",
      "<table><tr><td>298.15-1386.5 K:</td><td> $Cp^o$  = 11.534 + 1.806x $10^{-3}$ T - 0.223x $10^5$ T $^{-2}$ </td></tr><tr><td rowspan=\"3\">1386.5-1600 K:</td><td> $H^o$ -  $H_{298}^o$  = 11.534x $10^{-3}$ T + 0.903x $10^{-6}$ T $^2$  + 0.223x $10^2$ T $^{-1}$  - 3.594</td></tr><tr><td> $Cp^o$  = 16.000</td></tr><tr><td> $H^o$ -  $H_{298}^o$  = 16.000x $10^{-3}$ T - 3.534</td></tr></table>",
      "Reaction equations (kcal/mol):",
      "<table><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">298.15-600.65 K:</td><td> $\\Delta Hr^{\\circ} = -39.612 + 2.512 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T} - 1.021 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{T}^{2} + 29.550 \\text{T}^{-1}$ </td></tr><tr><td> $\\Delta Gr^{\\circ} = -39.612 - 2.512 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T} \\ln T + 1.021 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{T}^{2} + 14.775 \\text{T}^{-1} + 37.321 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T}$ </td></tr><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">600.65-1386.5 K:</td><td> $\\Delta Hr^{\\circ} = -39.960 - 0.052 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T} + 0.956 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{T}^{2} + 46.150 \\text{T}^{-1}$ </td></tr><tr><td> $\\Delta Gr^{\\circ} = -39.960 + 0.052 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T} \\ln T - 0.956 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{T}^{2} + 23.075 \\text{T}^{-1} + 22.660 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T}$ </td></tr><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">1386.5-1600 K:</td><td> $\\Delta Hr^{\\circ} = -39.900 + 4.414 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T} + 0.053 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{T}^{2} + 23.850 \\text{T}^{-1}$ </td></tr><tr><td> $\\Delta Gr^{\\circ} = -39.900 - 4.414 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T} \\ln T - 0.053 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{T}^{2} + 11.925 \\text{T}^{-1} + 53.681 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T}$ </td></tr></table>",
      "Sources: Enthalpy of formation at 298 K based on Kiukkola (69) and Thompson (117). Low-temperature heat capacities and entropy at 298 K based on Anderson (3), Parkinson (95), and Kelley (63). High-temperature data based on Thompson (116) and Blachnik (11). Temperature and enthalpy of fusion and heat capacity of liquid are those estimated by Chase (18)."
    ],
